<section
  id="case-heartasylum-logotype"
  class="fullscreen-section case-study-section"
  data-title="Case Study: Heart Asylum — Logo Suite &amp; Cover Art | Katja Turnsek"
  data-description="Graphic design case study for Heart Asylum, combining a dark-first logo system with atmospheric single-cover artwork for music and social platforms."
  data-case="heartasylum"
  aria-labelledby="case-heartasylum-logotype-title"
>
  <div class="rows">
    <div class="row">
      <div class="case-study-header">
        <p class="case-eyebrow">Graphic Design</p>
        <h1 id="case-heartasylum-logotype-title">Heart Asylum</h1>
        <p class="case-subtitle">A dark, flexible logo suite and atmospheric
          release artwork for an alternative rock duo.</p>

        <dl class="case-summary" aria-label="Project summary">
          <div>
            <dt>Role</dt>
            <dd>Logo design and cover artwork</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Scope</dt>
            <dd>Real-world music branding</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Deliverables</dt>
            <dd>Logo suite and single cover</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Tools</dt>
            <dd>Adobe Illustrator, Photoshop</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Outcome</dt>
            <dd>Release-ready visual identity</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>

        <div class="tech-demo-wrapper case-demo-wrapper">
          <div
            class="demo-links"
            data-auto="demo"
            data-case="heartasylum-logotype"
          ></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="row">
      <h2 class="case-section-heading">The brief</h2>
      <p>
        Heart Asylum needed a recognisable identity that matched the duo's dark,
        alternative sound and could move easily between avatars, streaming
        platforms, social graphics, posters, and release artwork. The single
        cover needed to extend the same atmosphere without making the logo or
        typography compete with the central image.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="row">
      <div class="case-study-grid">
        <article class="case-study-block case-study-block--challenge">
          <h2>Challenge</h2>
          <p>
            Music branding has to create mood immediately, but it also has to
            remain identifiable at the small sizes used by streaming and social
            platforms. The system therefore needed both expressive detail and a
            strong, simple silhouette.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Balance a dramatic visual tone with clarity at avatar size</li>
            <li>Support icon, wordmark, and full-lockup applications</li>
            <li>Connect the identity and cover artwork without repeating the
              same composition</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class="case-study-item case-study-media--explore">
          <img
            class="thumb"
            src="{{base}}assets/images/logo-heartasylum-logosuite-600.webp"
            data-src="logo-heartasylum-logosuite-600.webp"
            data-path="assets/images"
            width="600"
            height="389"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
            alt="Heart Asylum logo suite showing the primary lockup, heart icon, wordmark, secondary lockup, and reversed version"
          />
          <figcaption class="case-caption">Primary, secondary, icon, wordmark,
            and reversed logo variations</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <article class="case-study-block case-study-block--decisions">
          <h2>Key decisions</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Built the emblem around a heart with sharp, architectural linework
              to combine emotional meaning with a harder musical edge
            </li>
            <li>
              Created icon, wordmark, primary, secondary, and reversed versions
              so the identity can adapt without being redrawn for each format
            </li>
            <li>
              Used high-contrast wing linework as the cover's visual hook, with
              restrained typography and portrait detail supporting the mood
            </li>
            <li>
              Checked the square composition at reduced size so the wings,
              title, and band mark retain a clear hierarchy on streaming grids
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class="case-study-item case-study-media--booking">
          <img
            class="thumb"
            src="{{base}}assets/images/cover-heartasylum-used-to-believe-600.webp"
            data-src="cover-heartasylum-used-to-believe-600.webp"
            data-path="assets/images"
            width="600"
            height="600"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
            alt="Heart Asylum single cover for Used to Believe in Angels, with white wing linework over a dark portrait"
          />
          <figcaption class="case-caption">Used to Believe in Angels — square
            cover artwork designed for digital release</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="row case-outcome">
      <h2 class="case-section-heading">Outcome and reflection</h2>
      <p>
        The result is a cohesive release package rather than a standalone logo:
        a flexible identity for small digital placements and larger artwork,
        paired with a cover that carries the same dark tone in a more cinematic
        way. The project strengthened how I translate a musical atmosphere into
        practical assets that remain recognisable across formats.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="back-to-work-container">
      <a
